---
import { IS_TWITCH_ONLY } from "../../lib/constants";
---

<h2>1. Overview</h2>
<p>
    Mixchat is designed to function without a Mixchat-operated database for
    chat data. Account credentials live in <code>httpOnly</code> cookies, not
    in a server account store. Chat history is cached on your device. Logging
    out removes auth cookies and the UI session; clearing site data removes
    remaining local caches.
</p>
<p>
    A few opt-in streamer tools do write small JSON files on the server that
    runs Mixchat (the hosted website, or the local process in the desktop app):
    Song Queue, Wheel Spin, and Stream tools settings/state (giveaway
    entrants, music playlists, channel access). None of these store chat
    transcripts or login credentials. YouTube live chat is also relayed
    through that server in memory while you watch. See sections 2.2, 3.1, and
    4 below.
</p>

<h2>2. Data Collection</h2>

<h3>
    2.1 Logging In{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? " (Twitch)" : " (Twitch & YouTube)"}
</h3>
<p>
    When connecting a Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " or YouTube"} account, the
    browser gets "keys" (OAuth tokens) from{" "}
    {IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "the service" : "those services"}.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Secure Storage:</strong> Key tokens are stored as{" "}
        <code>httpOnly</code> cookies. This helps block outside scripts from
        seeing them.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>UI Session:</strong> A little bit of info (like your username
        and profile picture) is saved in <code>localStorage</code> so the app
        remembers who you are on refresh. The access token is stripped before
        saving; a provider refresh token may remain in that stored session.
        Clearing site data removes it.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>2.2 Your Messages</h3>
<p>
    Twitch chat reaches your browser over Twitch's own connections and is
    rendered from memory (with an optional local scrollback cache). YouTube
    live chat is proxied through the Mixchat server, which holds a short-lived
    in-memory copy (up to about 300 messages per open session, reaped after
    idle time) to fan messages out to your windows. Recent messages may also
    be cached on your device in IndexedDB for up to 5 days so you can scroll
    back; that cache is pruned automatically and is not uploaded.
</p>

{!IS_TWITCH_ONLY && (
    <>
        <h3>2.3 YouTube Integration</h3>
        <p>
            By linking a YouTube channel, Mixchat uses YouTube APIs with your
            sign-in to show live status and stream metadata, read and send
            live chat messages, and carry out moderation actions you trigger
            (delete a message, ban a viewer). Live chat delivery uses
            YouTube's internal (Innertube) endpoints in addition to the
            official Data API.
        </p>
    </>
)}

<h2>3. Third-Party Services</h2>
<p>To make everything work smoothly, Mixchat talks to a few core services:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " & YouTube"}:</strong> For the
        chat and video feeds.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Emote Providers (7TV, BTTV, FFZ):</strong> To render those
        custom emotes everyone loves. These services might see which channel
        you're watching so they can serve the right emote pack.
    </li>
    {!IS_TWITCH_ONLY && (
        <li>
            <strong>Google Drive (optional):</strong> If you enable settings
            backup, a JSON file of your settings can be saved to and restored
            from your own Google Drive. This is user-initiated and separate
            from chat. The backup can include stream-tool history keys such as
            past playlists (which may hold Song Queue owner tokens) and
            giveaway winner history.
        </li>
    )}
</ul>
<p>
    Other third parties the app may contact when you use it: a Twitch recent-
    message history relay (<code>recent-messages.robotty.de</code>), Google
    Fonts, Twemoji/Giphy CDNs, and embedded Twitch/YouTube players.
</p>
<p>Mixchat does not include analytics or telemetry.</p>

<h2>3.1 Song Queue Sessions</h2>
<p>
    When a streamer enables the Song Queue tool inside Stream tools on the
    hosted website, Mixchat creates a small server-side session record (a JSON
    file) that contains:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>The session title and a unique 24-character session id.</li>
    <li>
        The list of queued YouTube videos, including the submitter's display
        name (Twitch username or YouTube channel name) and avatar URL.
    </li>
    <li>
        A SHA-256 hash of the streamer's owner token. The owner token itself
        is returned to the streamer once and never stored on Mixchat servers.
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    If the streamer toggles <strong>Public link</strong> on, anyone with the
    link can read a stripped-down view of the queue (video ids, titles,
    submitter display names, thumbnails) at <code>/s/&lt;id&gt;</code>. The
    page does not show submitter user ids. It does show the queue title and a
    link to the streamer's channel name on Twitch or YouTube.
</p>
<p>
    No OAuth tokens, email addresses, or passwords are stored in the song
    queue record. Ending the queue makes it read-only and immediately turns
    off the public link; the session file is kept on the server so the
    streamer can browse past playlists, and it is removed only when deleted
    from that list. Song Queue and Wheel are not available in the desktop app
    UI.
</p>

<h2>4. Stateless and Database-Free (with One Exception)</h2>
<p>
    Mixchat's server/API is "stateless" for chat and OAuth data. This means it
    doesn't remember sessions from one second to the next without the browser
    sending its security key. There is no Mixchat backend database for
    messages, accounts, or emote data.
</p>
<p>
    The opt-in streamer tools are the stateful exception: Song Queue (section
    3.1), Wheel Spin wheels, and Stream tools settings/state (giveaway
    entrants and history, music playlists, channel access) are stored as
    small JSON files on the server that runs Mixchat. They are scoped to the
    streamer's channel. Ephemeral in-memory state also exists while open
    (for example YouTube chat fan-out and poll command relay).
</p>

<h2>5. Data Retention and Deletion</h2>
<p>
    <strong>Logout</strong> clears auth cookies and the UI session on this
    device. It does not by itself delete IndexedDB caches (such as the 5-day
    chat history) or other local settings.
</p>
<p>
    For a full local wipe on the website, clear site data for
    mixchat.grepfs.xyz in your browser settings.
</p>

<h2>6. Security</h2>
<p>
    Login tokens are managed using secure HTTP-only cookies. It is recommended
    to use strong passwords and two-factor authentication (2FA) on Twitch
    {IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " and YouTube"} accounts.
</p>
